From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] soundwire: qcom: do not enumerate before the bus knows its slaves

The second consecutive DSP restart in a boot loses the sound card:

    sysfs: cannot create duplicate filename '/bus/soundwire/devices/sdw:1:0:0217:0204:00:0'
      sdw_slave_add <- sdw_of_find_slaves <- sdw_bus_master_add <- qcom_swrm_probe
    soundwire sdw-master-1-0: Failed to add slave: ret -17
    /proc/asound/cards -> --- no soundcards ---

and leaves a driverless device behind, its status attribute empty, where a
wsa884x should be.

qcom_swrm_get_device_status() answers an attach by searching bus->slaves for a
matching device id and, when it finds none, registering the device itself with
a NULL fwnode -- deliberately, so an undeclared part is still visible in
sysfs. But qcom_swrm_probe() requested the interrupt well before
sdw_bus_master_add(), and it is sdw_bus_master_add() that fills bus->slaves
from the device tree. An attach arriving in between therefore searches an
empty list, always "finds none", and claims the name the device tree entry is
about to want; sdw_of_find_slaves() then collides with it and returns -EEXIST.

A first probe never hits this, because nothing is attached yet and enumeration
only starts when qcom_swrm_init() programs the block. On a re-probe the parts
are still enumerated on the wire from the previous incarnation, so the
interrupt can fire the moment it is requested. Nothing on this SoC used to
re-probe these masters, which is why it has sat here unexercised; it becomes
reachable as soon as a DSP restart takes the SoundWire masters down and brings
them back, and it needs two restarts in one boot to show.

So request the interrupt after sdw_bus_master_add(). Enumeration is driven by
qcom_swrm_init(), which runs later still, so nothing needs the handler any
earlier.

Close the same window on the way out as well. sdw_bus_master_delete() empties
bus->slaves while the interrupt is still live -- devres does not free it until
after remove() returns -- so an attach landing there re-registers the device
that was just deleted and the next probe inherits the collision. disable_irq()
before the delete; devres still frees it afterwards, and 0103's dispose action
still runs after that.

Incidentally fixes a clock leak on the way past: the wake-irq failure path
jumped to err_init, which returns without the clk_disable_unprepare() that
err_clk does. It now unwinds through err_master_add.

---
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -1655,6 +1655,38 @@
 
 	ctrl->reg_read(ctrl, SWRM_COMP_HW_VERSION, &ctrl->version);
 
+	ctrl->bus.controller_id = -1;
+
+	if (ctrl->version > SWRM_VERSION_1_3_0) {
+		ctrl->reg_read(ctrl, SWRM_COMP_MASTER_ID, &val);
+		ctrl->bus.controller_id = val;
+	}
+
+	ret = sdw_bus_master_add(&ctrl->bus, dev, dev->fwnode);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(dev, "Failed to register Soundwire controller (%d)\n",
+			ret);
+		goto err_clk;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Only now that sdw_bus_master_add() has put the device tree's slaves
+	 * on bus->slaves. qcom_swrm_get_device_status() answers an attach by
+	 * searching that list and, finding nothing, registering the device
+	 * itself with a NULL fwnode -- so an interrupt arriving while the list
+	 * is still empty claims the name the device tree entry is about to
+	 * want, and sdw_of_find_slaves() then fails:
+	 *
+	 *   sysfs: cannot create duplicate filename '/bus/soundwire/devices/sdw:1:0:0217:0204:00:0'
+	 *   soundwire sdw-master-1-0: Failed to add slave: ret -17
+	 *
+	 * leaving a driverless device behind and no sound card. A first probe
+	 * never hits it -- nothing is attached yet -- but on a re-probe the
+	 * parts are still enumerated on the wire from the previous incarnation
+	 * and the interrupt can fire immediately. Enumeration is driven from
+	 * qcom_swrm_init() below, so nothing needs the handler before this
+	 * point.
+	 */
 	ret = devm_request_threaded_irq(dev, ctrl->irq, NULL,
 					qcom_swrm_irq_handler,
 					IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING |
@@ -1662,7 +1694,7 @@
 					"soundwire", ctrl);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "Failed to request soundwire irq\n");
-		goto err_clk;
+		goto err_master_add;
 	}
 
 	ctrl->wake_irq = of_irq_get(dev->of_node, 1);
@@ -1673,24 +1705,10 @@
 						"swr_wake_irq", ctrl);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(dev, "Failed to request soundwire wake irq\n");
-			goto err_init;
+			goto err_master_add;
 		}
 	}
 
-	ctrl->bus.controller_id = -1;
-
-	if (ctrl->version > SWRM_VERSION_1_3_0) {
-		ctrl->reg_read(ctrl, SWRM_COMP_MASTER_ID, &val);
-		ctrl->bus.controller_id = val;
-	}
-
-	ret = sdw_bus_master_add(&ctrl->bus, dev, dev->fwnode);
-	if (ret) {
-		dev_err(dev, "Failed to register Soundwire controller (%d)\n",
-			ret);
-		goto err_clk;
-	}
-
 	qcom_swrm_init(ctrl);
 	wait_for_completion_timeout(&ctrl->enumeration,
 				    msecs_to_jiffies(TIMEOUT_MS));
@@ -1728,6 +1746,16 @@
 {
 	struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl = dev_get_drvdata(&pdev->dev);
 
+	/*
+	 * The other half of the race the probe path comments on: the handler
+	 * registers a slave it cannot find on bus->slaves, and
+	 * sdw_bus_master_delete() empties that list while the interrupt is
+	 * still live -- devres does not free it until after this returns. An
+	 * attach landing in that window re-registers the device that was just
+	 * deleted, and the next probe inherits the collision.
+	 */
+	disable_irq(ctrl->irq);
+
 	sdw_bus_master_delete(&ctrl->bus);
 
 	/*
